"_____________________________________________________________"
"The JSON Library in Bern"
"Parse JSON text into Bern values, and serialize Bern values back to JSON."
"_____________________________________________________________\n"
import json

"[1. Parsing objects]"
"json_parse builds an Object from a JSON object; index it with [key]."

"\nResult:"
person = json_parse("{\"name\": \"Bern\", \"version\": 3}")
person["name"]
person["version"]

"\n_____________________________________________________________\n"
"[2. Arrays become Sets, with duplicate grouping]"
"A JSON array maps to a Set. A value that appears once stays bare; a value"
"that repeats collapses into a sub-list of its copies. This keeps set theory"
"intact while preserving multiplicity. Nested arrays stay Sets, so a List"
"inside the set always means 'grouped duplicates'."
"Input:  [1, a, 34.2, a, banana]   ->   {1, [a, a], 34.2, banana}"

"\nResult:"
json_parse("[1, \"a\", 34.2, \"a\", \"banana\"]")

"\n_____________________________________________________________\n"
"[3. null becomes Undefined]"
"Use is_null to test for a JSON null."

"\nResult:"
is_null(json_parse("null"))

"\n_____________________________________________________________\n"
"[4. Serializing with json_stringify]"
"json_stringify turns a Bern value back into a JSON string."

"\nResult:"
json_stringify(json_parse("{\"items\": [1, 2, 3], \"ok\": true}"))

"\n_____________________________________________________________\n"
"[5. Round-tripping]"
"Duplicate groups expand back to repeated array elements."
"json_stringify(json_parse(\"[7, 7, 7]\"))"

"\nResult:"
json_stringify(json_parse("[7, 7, 7]"))
